JLS(1)                         ZOAU Command Syntax                        JLS(1)

NAME

    jls - List jobs that match the provided owner and prefix.

SYNOPSIS

    jls [-dhjJlv] [-o <field list>] '[/owner][/job]'

    -d
        Print debug information. (changes output)

    -h
        Display syntax help.

    -j
        Print output in JSON format in this form:
            { "data": { "JOBXXXXX":{...} }, "meta": {...} }

        Each object will have only the fields requested with the
        -o or -l options.

    -J
        Prints JSON output in a readable format.
        This option forces -j if it was not set.

    -l
        Print additional fields.
        Note: this option may take a long time to process.

    -v
        Print verbose information and a list header.

    -o <field list>
        The output list will include only the fields specified in <field list>.
        Order of <field list> is maintained. Names are case insensitive.

    Note: If both -l and -o options are enabled, -l will be ignored.

DESCRIPTION

    jls lists all jobs that match the provided filter.

    Note: The jls utility lists jobs exclusively from the single active JES address 
    space within the LPAR where it is executed. Cross-JES routing is not supported 
    for listing jobs on remote sysplex nodes.

    Output has the following fields by default:
        <owner> <name> <id> <status> <ccode>

    Additionally, these fields are appended when using the -l option:
        <jobclass> <serviceclass> <priority> <asid> <creationdate>
        <creationtime> <queueposition> <programname> <jobtype> <executiontime>
        <executionseconds> <system> <subsystem> <onode> <xnode> <membname>
        <cputime> <srbtime>

    A value of '?' represents an unknown value for that particular field.
    It can take some time for a job to have all of its fields populated.
    Try again in a few seconds if '?' is an unexpected response.

    <owner>
        Owner of the job (e.g. IBMUSER)
        '?' returned if unknown or pending

    <name>
        Name of the job (e.g. IBMUSER1)
        '?' returned if unknown or pending

    <id>
        ID of the job (e.g. JOB12345)
        '?' returned if unknown or pending

    <status> can be one of:
        ABEND       : Ended abnormally.
        AC          : Active.
        CAB         : Converter abend.
        CANCELED    : Job was canceled.
        CC          : Completed normally.
        CNV         : Converter error.
        EOM         : Job failed in EOM.
        FLU         : Job has been flushed.
        HOLD        : Job is being held.
        JCLERR      : Job had a JCL error.
        NOEXEC      : Job has SCAN or COPY JCL run type.
        SEC         : Security error.
        SYS         : System failure.
        ?           : Error or unknown status.

    <ccode> can be one of:
        <number>        : Maximum condition code of all job steps.
        <alpha-numeric> : Abend code of step that abended.
        ?               : Job is running or did not execute. Code is unknown.

    <jobclass>
        Job class.

    <serviceclass>
        WLM service class. This value is only available if any of the following are true:
          - Job is awaiting execution.
          - Job is scheduled to a WLM managed job class.
          - Job is not held (duplicate job name, operator hold, etc.)
          - Member it has affinity to is available.
          - The scheduling environment is available.
        '?' returned if unknown or pending.

    <priority>
        Job priority.

    <ASID>
        The Address Space Identifier (ASID) is a unique descriptor for the job address space.
        Zero if not active.

     <creationdate>
        Input start date in format YYYY/MM/DD.

     <creationtime>
        Input start time in format HH:MM:SS.

    <queueposition>
        Position of job on class queue or phase queue.

    <programname>
        This field is only valid for JES2 systems; in JES3 systems the value is
        always '?'. In JES2, this field displays the program name from the job's
        last completed step, which is derived from the PGM= parameter on the
        EXEC card.

    <jobtype>
        Type of address space. Can be one of:
          APPC  : APPC Initiator.
          JGRP  : JOBGROUP.
          JOB   : Batch job.
          STC   : Started task.
          TSU   : Time sharing user.
          ?     : Unknown or pending.

    <executiontime>
        Elapsed execution time in the format [ddd-]HH:MM:SS.
        The length of this field is always 12 characters. The first four
        characters are blank if the elapsed time is less than 24 hours.
          ddd-  : Number of 24 hour segments (days) if the job ran longer than 24 hours.
                  The dash character separates elapsed days from hours.
                  The maximum value that can be displayed is 999 days. Jobs that
                  run longer will continue to display this value.
          HH    : Number of hours (00 to 23).
          MM    : Number of minutes (00 to 59).
          SS    : Number of seconds (00 to 59).
        The value is '?' in any of the following cases:
          - Job is awaiting execution.
          - Job did not execute due to an error.

    <executionseconds>
        Number of seconds the job executed for.
        The value is '?' in any of the following cases:
          - Job is awaiting execution.
          - Job did not execute due to an error.

    <system>
        MVS system name where the job ran.
        The value is '?' in any of the following cases:
          - Job is awaiting execution.
          - Job did not execute due to an error.
          - Job did not execute due to its TYPRUN value.

    <subsystem>
        Name of the subsystem that executed the job.

    <onode>
        Origin node (node that submitted the job).

    <xnode>
        Execution node.

    <membname>
        Execution JES member name.
        The value is '?' in any of the following cases:
          - Job is awaiting execution.
          - Job did not execute due to an error.
          - Job did not execute due to its TYPRUN value.

    <cputime>
        Sum of the CPU time used by each job step, in microseconds.
        The value is '?' in any of the following cases:
          - Job is awaiting execution.
          - Job did not execute due to an error.

    <srbtime>
        Sum of the service request block (SRB) time for each job step, in microseconds.
        The value is '?' in any of the following cases:
          - Job is awaiting execution.
          - Job did not execute due to an error.

EXAMPLES

    Shows jobs with no owner/prefix specification:

        jls

        jls '/*'

    Shows caller's jobs:

        jls '*'

    Show caller's jobs with additional fields and a header:

        jls -lv '*'

    Shows all jobs for IBMUSER:

        jls '/IBMUSER'

    Shows caller's job JOB01108:

        jls 'job01108'

    Show the ID, name, status, and job class for job JOB01108:

        jls -o id,name,status,jobclass 'job01108'

    Show all fields from the -l option (except for programname) for
    the caller's jobs, with a header:

        jls -v -o owner,name,id,status,ccode,jobclass,serviceclass,priority,asid,creationdate,creationtime,queueposition,jobtype,executiontime '*'

    Get a JSON string with name, owner, and creation date fields:

        jls -o name,owner,creationdate -j '*'

    Show the status and execution time for active job J0000604, with a header:

        jls -v -o id,status,ccode,executiontime,executionseconds J0000604

        The job is currently active, and the command produces the following output:

        id       status   ccode executiontime executionseconds
        J0000604 AC           ?      00:01:04               64

    Show the name, status, and execution time for long-running SYSLOG jobs:

        jls -o id,name,status,ccode,executiontime,executionseconds '//SYSLOG'

        The jobs have been running for over three days, and the command produces
        the following output:

        id       name     status   ccode executiontime executionseconds
        S0000003 SYSLOG   AC           ?    3-03:39:59           272399
        S0000002 SYSLOG   AC           ?    3-03:40:35           272435
        S0000001 SYSLOG   AC           ?    3-03:49:31           272971

    Show the status and execution time for a complete job:

        jls -v -o id,ccode,executiontime,executionseconds J0000271

        The job ran for a short time, and the command produces the following output:

        id       status   ccode executiontime executionseconds
        J0000271 CC        0000      00:00:05                5


EXIT VALUES

    0
        Success

    non-zero
        Error occurred. See error messages for details.

SEE ALSO

    ddls(1) jsub(1) jcan(1) pjdd(1)
